{
  "id": "meditation-prep",
  "name": "Meditation prep / temporal session (both sides)",
  "category": "meditation",
  "evidence": "anecdote",
  "origin": "Todd Murphy (Shakti meditation advice; temporal sessions of the Feelgood and lucid-dream recipes)",
  "uT": 1,
  "summary": "Murphy's temporal session: the amygdala and hippocampal signals one after the other, with 4-s pauses, over both temporal lobes for 30 min, as preparation for meditation an hour or two later. Murphy: don't meditate during the stimulation. It is also the temporal session of his lucid-dream series (five weekly sessions before the frontal one).",
  "notes": "For Vipassana or Zen, Murphy suggests 30-min Feelgood or 'altered states' sessions over both sides, or hippocampal signals over the right side only, and practising \"an hour to 90 minutes later\". This preset is the temporal part those bilateral sessions share: phase 1 of the Feelgood session and sessions 1-5 of the lucid-dream series play the amygdala and hippocampal signals in sequence over both temporal lobes. Two coils per side (FT7, TP7 and FT8, TP8 on CH1-4, so the same 8-coil set-up runs the frontal preset on CH5-8); the vendor shows only photos, so the site names are an inference. The signals are proprietary. One pass here is: the amygdala signal, approximated by the built-in accelerating burst (6 to 30 Hz, 230 points of 3 ms = 0.69 s; the 230-point length is from Ryczko & Persinger 2002); 4 s of silence (the vendor: the two signals are \"generated one after the other ... with a four-second latency\"); the hippocampal 'LTP' signal, approximated by four theta bursts of four 100 Hz pulses (Larson & Lynch timing) repeated at 5.4 Hz, the spectral peak the vendor reports for it (0.6 s, with 1-ms points instead of the vendor's 3-ms points so the 100 Hz pulses fit); then 4 s of silence again. That gives about 8 s of field per minute (the vendor states 9.6 s). Meditating or using other mind machines during the session \"gives your brain conflicting instructions\", Murphy says; use other mind machines about 2 h later, and no more than 3-5 a day. An independent sham-controlled test of the 8 Coil Shakti amygdala signal over both temporal lobes (30 min) found no effect on emotional responses (Gendle & McGrath 2012), and Granqvist 2005 found no field effect. Persinger's lab reported more aggression and less trust after three weekly bilateral burst-firing sessions (Gillis & Persinger 1993): stop if you notice irritability. Not for people with epilepsy, migraine, a psychiatric diagnosis or medication.",
  "setting": "No darkness or sensory deprivation needed; seated, eyes closed, relaxed but not meditating. Meditate 60-120 min after the session ends (Murphy: \"an hour to 90 minutes later\").",
  "schedule": "Weekly or every 72 h, never on consecutive days; several sessions may be needed before an effect is noticed. Series of six, then a 21-day break. Lucid-dream series: five weekly sessions, then the frontal preset.",
  "targets": ["left-amygdala", "right-amygdala", "left-hippocampus-body", "right-hippocampus-body"],
  "source": "Murphy, Meditation and the 8 Coil Shakti https://www.god-helmet.com/wp/winshakti/rotating/shakti_meditation.htm ; Murphy, Shakti demo: During sessions https://www.god-helmet.com/wp/winshakti/demo/shakti_demo/during_sessions.htm ; Murphy, 8 Coil Shakti safety https://www.god-helmet.com/wp/winshakti/rotating/rot_safety.htm ; Murphy, Mood enhancement and the 8 Coil Shakti https://www.god-helmet.com/wp/winshakti/rotating/shakti_happiness.htm ; Murphy, Lucid dreaming and the 8 Coil Shakti https://www.god-helmet.com/wp/winshakti/rotating/lucid_dream.htm ; Murphy, Shakti demo: What are 'Rapid' and 'High-Speed' signals? https://www.god-helmet.com/wp/winshakti/demo/shakti_demo/rapid_&_high_speed_signals.htm ; Murphy, Deja vu lecture https://youtu.be/KJIAHHB8Btk?t=3861 ; Ryczko & Persinger 2002, Percept Mot Skills 95:592 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12434855/ ; Gillis & Persinger 1993, Percept Mot Skills 76:168 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8451123/ ; Gendle & McGrath 2012, Percept Mot Skills 114:217 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22582690/ ; Granqvist et al. 2005, Neurosci Lett 379:1 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15849873/",
